#include <iostream>

// Base case for recursion (handles an empty pack)
void printAll() {
    std::cout << "\n";
}

// Recursive variadic function
template<typename T, typename... Args>
void printAll(T first, Args... rest) {
    std::cout << first << " ";
    printAll(rest...); // Pack expansion in a function call
}

// Example usage:
// printAll(1, 2.5, "hello", 'C');
```

Pack expansion is the mechanism by which a parameter pack is expanded into a sequence of individual arguments. It's denoted by `...` following the name of the pack or an expression involving the pack.

Example:
```cpp
template<typename... Args>
void forwardArgs(Args... args) {
    // 'args...' expands the pack into individual arguments
    // e.g., if args was (int, double), this becomes (int_val, double_val)
    someOtherFunction(args...);
}
```

A fold expression provides a concise syntax to apply a binary operator over all elements of a parameter pack. It simplifies operations that would traditionally require explicit recursive variadic templates.

Example (unary left fold to sum):
```cpp
template<typename... Args>
auto sumAll(Args... args) {
    // (args + ...) expands to (arg1 + (arg2 + (... + argN)))
    // Requires at least one argument. Will fail for empty pack.
    return (args + ...);
}

// Example with an initial value (binary left fold):
template<typename... Args>
auto sumAllWithInitial(Args... args) {
    // (0 + ... + args) expands to (((0 + arg1) + arg2) + ... + argN)
    // Works correctly even for an empty pack (returns 0).
    return (0 + ... + args);
}
```
